esting whether the diagonal neighbor being empty versus filled determines the 2 vs 4 assignment, but both cases show empty diagonals, so that alone can't distinguish them - I need to think about convexity along the path direction instead, checking whether the turn is an exterior or interior angle relative to the shape's boundary.\n\nI'm mapping out the nonzero column positions row by row for the test grid to reconstruct the shape's outline.\n\nContinuing to map r14, I'm now suspecting this whole grid forms a single closed loop rather than separate segments, so I'm tracing the path cell by cell starting from (1,2) to verify connectivity.\n\nI'm listing out the full path coordinates in order to compute the signed area and determine orientation, so I can tell convex from reflex corners at each vertex.\n\nTracing through the full boundary confirms it's a clockwise loop with the interior on the right side. Now I'm establishing the convention for classifying corners: since I'm working in image coordinates with rows increasing downward, a right turn (going right then turning down) should correspond to a convex vertex in this clockwise traversal, while left turns are reflex.\n\nI need to check each corner against the actual interior region \u2014 like at (1,4) where the path turns from right to down, I need to verify whether the diagonal neighbor cell (2,3) is truly interior to the shape, since that determines whether this corner counts as convex or reflex.\n\nColumn1 has a notch shape at row6 where the boundary dips - checking whether that gap connects to the interior or stays part of the exterior, and it looks like it's isolated and only touches the outside region, so it's fine.\n\nNow tracing whether the big interior region leaks anywhere - checking rows 7 through 9 column by column to confirm the open cells all link into one contiguous blob bounded properly by X's above and below, and so far everything checks out.\n\nThen I'm listing out the full interior region: rows 3